# Build-context exclusions (BuildKit reads this <Dockerfile>.dockerignore; the classic builder reads the
# context-root .dockerignore, which is kept in sync with this file). The build context is the project
# root (compose sets `context: ..`). Keep the image to CODE plus the shipped config TEMPLATES only —
# never a developer's runtime state or real secrets.

.git/
.vscode/

# Never copy the host's node_modules into the image: it may be built for a different OS/arch (e.g. macOS)
# and would break the Alpine runtime. The Dockerfile installs dependencies cleanly inside the image.
node_modules/

# Runtime / generated state. These are Docker named volumes at runtime, and a fresh volume seeds from the
# image on first run — so baking a developer's copy in would ship logs, backups, uploads and cached data
# into a brand-new install. Excluding them means a fresh volume starts empty and the app creates what it
# needs.
data/
logs/
backups/
uploads/
rollbacks/
temp/
downloads/

# Per-instance identity. Build images from the shipped config TEMPLATES — an app.json with an empty
# mongo_db_url, a credential-free bot.json, and (for a future Hub build) a hub.json template — never a
# populated working copy, otherwise the image would carry your exchange keys and app-password hash. Only
# this host's identity file is excluded; the config templates are intentionally kept so a fresh install
# (instance or Hub) can seed from them.
config/server.json

# Dev-only tooling and tests the runtime never needs (and which are not part of the deployed project).
.githooks/
scripts/
libs/test/
symbot-backlog.md
